;;; A kernel dunder a program writes as a value, among the blind enumerator's
;;; objects — **W96**, register row `W96/as-value`
;;; (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A rule with two relation parameters, `lift`, handed `__closed__` as its
;;; second: `(lift complete __closed__)`, as `std.algebra` writes `(imply1
;;; functional __closed__)`. With `(complete thing)` it derives `(__closed__
;;; thing)`. The dunder is written in an argument position of a fact, which
;;; is legal and stays so, and no file writes a fact headed by it. Beside it,
;;; a relation the program declares and does not close, `total`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The hypotheses are the facts of `total` over the things the puzzle is
;;; about, and it is about one, `a`. `__closed__` is the kernel's word,
;;; written here to say which relation the rule concludes: no object, however
;;; it is written. One candidate, `(total a)`, and R two states.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two candidates, `(total __closed__)` and `(total a)`, and R four states:
;;; a constant in an argument position of a fact is a name of the program,
;;; and the blind enumerator takes every such name no registry holds for an
;;; object.

(relation thing Thing :why "{?1} is a thing")
(thing a)
(rule lift (?P ?Q) :match (?P ?r) :assert (?Q ?r)
  :why "{?r} is {?P}, so it is {?Q}")
(lift complete __closed__)
(complete thing)
(relation total Number :why "the total is {?1}")
(query :goal (total ?x))
